{% extends "layouts/docs" %}

{% block content %}
	<p>Kiln has a language server that gives any editor that supports the Language Server Protocol errors as you type, formatting, completions and more. There are ready-made extensions for VS Code and Zed.</p>

	<h2>VS Code</h2>

	<p>Search for <strong>Kiln</strong> in the Extensions view. The extension is on the Visual Studio Marketplace and on Open VSX, so it also works in Cursor, Windsurf and VSCodium. It adds:</p>

	<ul>
		<li>Syntax highlighting for Kiln and the HTML around it, including Kiln inside attribute values, with JavaScript in <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> and CSS in <code>&lt;style&gt;</code>.</li>
		<li>A file icon for <code>.kiln</code> files, which shows when your icon theme doesn't have one.</li>
		<li>Comment toggling with <code>&#123;# #}</code>, bracket matching, auto-closing for <code>&#123;{</code> and <code>&#123;%</code>, folding for block tags, and indenting as you type.</li>
		<li>Snippets for each block tag, such as <code>for</code> and <code>component</code>.</li>
		<li>The language server, which comes with the extension and runs on VS Code's own Node.</li>
	</ul>

	<p>VS Code formats with the language server. To format when you save, add this to your settings. The <code>editor.defaultFormatter</code> line matters if your settings pick a formatter for every language, such as Biome or Prettier, which can't format Kiln.</p>

	{% apply highlight("json") %}
		{% verbatim %}
{
	"[kiln]": {
		"editor.defaultFormatter": "buildlucid.kiln",
		"editor.formatOnSave": true
	}
}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>VS Code's indentation settings decide whether the formatter indents with tabs or spaces. The extension defaults to tabs.</p>

	<p>To use Tailwind CSS class completion and Emmet abbreviations in templates, treat Kiln as HTML in both:</p>

	{% apply highlight("json") %}
		{% verbatim %}
{
	"tailwindCSS.includeLanguages": { "kiln": "html" },
	"emmet.includeLanguages": { "kiln": "html" }
}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2>Zed</h2>

	<p>Install the <strong>Kiln</strong> extension from the extensions page (<code>zed: extensions</code>). It adds:</p>

	<ul>
		<li>Syntax highlighting for Kiln and the HTML around it, with JavaScript in <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> and CSS in <code>&lt;style&gt;</code>.</li>
		<li>Comment toggling with <code>&#123;# #}</code>, bracket matching, and auto-closing for <code>&#123;{</code> and <code>&#123;%</code>.</li>
		<li>Indenting as you type, and an outline of blocks, macros, components and slots.</li>
		<li>Snippets for each block tag, such as <code>for</code> and <code>component</code>.</li>
		<li>The language server, which Zed installs and starts for you. It needs Node, which Zed provides.</li>
	</ul>

	<p>Zed formats with the language server. To format when you save, add this to your settings. The <code>formatter</code> line matters if your settings pick a formatter for every language, such as Biome or Prettier, which can't format Kiln.</p>

	{% apply highlight("json") %}
		{% verbatim %}
{
	"languages": {
		"Kiln": {
			"formatter": "language_server",
			"format_on_save": "on"
		}
	}
}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Zed's tab settings decide whether the formatter indents with tabs or spaces. The extension defaults to tabs.</p>

	<p>To use Tailwind CSS class completion in templates, add Kiln to the Tailwind language server's languages:</p>

	{% apply highlight("json") %}
		{% verbatim %}
{
	"languages": {
		"Kiln": {
			"language_servers": ["kiln", "tailwindcss-language-server", "..."]
		}
	},
	"lsp": {
		"tailwindcss-language-server": {
			"settings": {
				"includeLanguages": { "kiln": "html" }
			}
		}
	}
}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<h2>Other editors</h2>

	<p>Install the language server from npm and point your editor at it. It talks over standard input and output.</p>

	{% apply highlight("sh") %}
		{% verbatim %}
npm install --save-dev @lucidkiln/language-server
npx kiln-language-server --stdio
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Register it for files ending in <code>.kiln</code>. Kiln's syntax is close to Twig's, so a Twig highlighting grammar is a reasonable stand-in until your editor has a Kiln one.</p>

	<h2>What the language server does</h2>

	<ul>
		<li><strong>Errors as you type.</strong> The same problems <a href="/docs/api/errors/#checking-templates"><code>check</code></a> reports, from syntax errors to props a component doesn't declare.</li>
		<li><strong>Formatting.</strong> The whole file or a selection, using the <a href="/docs/tooling/formatting/">formatter</a>.</li>
		<li><strong>Completions</strong> for tags, filters, functions, tests, variables in scope, <code>loop</code>'s properties, template names in <code>extends</code>, <code>include</code>, <code>component</code> and <code>import</code>, the props of the component or template you're passing data to, and properties after a dot when the language server knows the value's type. Tags and built-ins show the same docs as hover.</li>
		<li><strong>Hover</strong> for tags, filters, functions, tests, variables and templates. Tags and built-ins show their docs from this site, up to the first example, with a link to the section. End tags, such as <code>endfor</code>, show the tag they close. A template shows its props. Your own functions, filters, tests, globals and typed values show their type and doc comment from <a href="#types-from-typescript">TypeScript</a>.</li>
		<li><strong>HTML completions and hover</strong> outside <code>&#123;{ }}</code> and <code>&#123;% %}</code>: elements, attributes and their values, such as the values of <code>button</code>'s <code>type</code>, with MDN's description and a link to its page. They come from the HTML service VS Code uses.</li>
		<li><strong>Signature help</strong> for functions and filters: their parameters as you type the arguments, with the one you're on highlighted.</li>
		<li><strong>Go to definition</strong> for template names, macros, blocks and variables. A variable goes to the prop, <code>set</code>, <code>for</code>, macro argument or import that brings it into scope. Your own functions, filters, tests, globals and the properties of typed values go to their <a href="#types-from-typescript">TypeScript</a> source.</li>
		<li><strong>Links</strong> on template names, and a list of the template's blocks, macros and slots for your editor's outline.</li>
	</ul>

	<p>The language server uses the version of Kiln installed in your project when there is one, so its errors match how your templates render.</p>

	<h2>Telling the editor about your project</h2>

	<p>The language server reads its settings from a <code>kiln.json</code> file. It uses the nearest one in the template's folder or any folder above it.</p>

	<p>Checks use unsaved text from every open template, including after <code>kiln.json</code> changes. Template discovery skips symlinks, hidden folders and <code>node_modules</code> without entering them.</p>

	{% apply highlight("json") %}
		{% verbatim %}
{
	"root": "src/templates",
	"strict": true,
	"functions": ["documents", "document"],
	"filters": ["markdown"],
	"tests": [],
	"globals": ["site"],
	"types": ["Post"]
}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><code>root</code></td><td>The folder template names are relative to, from the folder holding <code>kiln.json</code>. Default <code>"."</code>.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>strict</code></td><td>Whether your environment uses <a href="/docs/language/strict-mode/">strict mode</a>. Default <code>true</code>.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>functions</code>, <code>filters</code>, <code>tests</code></td><td>The names of the functions, filters and tests your project registers, on top of Kiln's own.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>globals</code></td><td>The names of your globals.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>types</code></td><td>The names of the prop types you register in the <code>types</code> option, such as <code>Post</code>.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>htmlChecks</code></td><td>Your <a href="/docs/tooling/html-checks/#turning-rules-off"><code>htmlChecks</code></a> option. Default <code>true</code>.</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<p>The editor never runs your code. It only needs the names. Without a <code>kiln.json</code>, the language server can't tell which names your project adds or where template names start, so it reports syntax errors and <a href="/docs/tooling/html-checks/">HTML checks</a> only, and go to definition stays inside the current template.</p>

	<h3>Generating kiln.json</h3>

	<p>Rather than keep the file up to date by hand, write it from your environment with <code>generateManifest</code>, next to <a href="/docs/api/types/"><code>generateTypes</code></a>. Pass the template folder relative to where you write the file.</p>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
import { writeFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { createKiln, generateManifest } from "@lucidkiln/core";
import { fileLoader } from "@lucidkiln/core/node";

const kiln = createKiln({ loader: fileLoader({ root: "src/templates" }), functions, filters });

await writeFile("kiln.json", generateManifest(kiln, { root: "src/templates" }));
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Names are sorted, so the file only changes when you add or remove one.</p>

	<h2>Types from TypeScript</h2>

	<p>If your project uses TypeScript, the language server can read the types of your functions, filters, tests, globals and prop types. With them, it:</p>

	<ul>
		<li>Completes properties after a dot, such as <code>post.</code>, or <code>brick.</code> inside a loop over <code>page.bricks</code>.</li>
		<li>Shows the type and doc comment of your functions, filters, tests, globals and their properties when you hover over them.</li>
		<li>Goes to the TypeScript source of a function, filter, test, global or property.</li>
		<li>Shows your function's or filter's parameters and their types as you type its arguments, and completes <a href="/docs/language/expressions/#calling-functions">named arguments</a>, such as <code>limit:</code>.</li>
		<li>Warns when an argument doesn't fit the parameter's type, such as text where a number belongs, or when a named argument isn't one the function takes.</li>
	</ul>

	<p>The editor still never runs your code. It asks your project's own copy of TypeScript what the types are.</p>

	<h3>Describing your functions</h3>

	<p>Add your functions, filters, tests and globals to Kiln's interfaces through module augmentation, the same way as <a href="/docs/api/types/#your-own-types">project-wide prop types</a>:</p>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
import { createKiln } from "@lucidkiln/core";
import { fileLoader } from "@lucidkiln/core/node";

const functions = {
	/** Finds the published documents in a collection, newest first. */
	documents: async (collection: string, query: { limit?: number } = {}) =>
		db.list(collection, query),
};
const filters = { markdown };
const globals = { site: { name: "Kiln", url: "https://kilnlang.com" } };

export const kiln = createKiln({
	loader: fileLoader({ root: "src/templates" }),
	functions,
	filters,
	globals,
	types: { Post: (value) => value instanceof PostView },
});

type Functions = typeof functions;
type Filters = typeof filters;
type Globals = typeof globals;

declare module "@lucidkiln/core" {
	interface KilnFunctions extends Functions {}
	interface KilnFilters extends Filters {}
	interface KilnGlobals extends Globals {}
	interface KilnTypes {
		Post: PostView;
	}
}
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<ul>
		<li>The interfaces are <code>KilnFunctions</code>, <code>KilnFilters</code>, <code>KilnTests</code>, <code>KilnGlobals</code> and <code>KilnTypes</code>.</li>
		<li>They only describe your project to tools. <code>createKiln</code> doesn't check them against what you register, so keep them next to the code that registers your functions.</li>
		<li>More than one file can add to them. A plugin can describe the functions it registers, and you add yours.</li>
		<li>Keep each function's own type. A map typed as <code>Record&lt;string, KilnCallable&gt;</code> tells the editor only that each entry is some function.</li>
		<li>For a function wrapped in <a href="/docs/api/extending/#request-data"><code>withRender</code></a>, the editor leaves out the first argument, which Kiln passes for you.</li>
		<li>For a filter, the first argument is the value before the <code>|</code>.</li>
		<li>Prop types come from <code>KilnTypes</code>. If you pass your type map to the generated <code>Schema</code> instead, add it to <code>KilnTypes</code> too, with <code>interface KilnTypes extends HostTypes {}</code>.</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>Generic functions</h3>

	<p>When what a function returns depends on its arguments, make it generic. The editor hands TypeScript the arguments from the template, so TypeScript works out the type parameters for each call.</p>

	{% apply highlight("ts") %}
		{% verbatim %}
type Collections = { pages: PageView; posts: PostView };

const functions = {
	documents: <Key extends keyof Collections>(collection: Key): Promise<Collections[Key][]> =>
		db.list(collection),
};
		{% endverbatim %}
	{% endapply %}

	<p>Then <code>{% verbatim %}{% set posts = documents("posts") %}{% endverbatim %}</code> makes <code>posts</code> a list of <code>PostView</code>, and <code>documents("pages")</code> a list of <code>PageView</code>. Functions wrapped in <code>withRender</code> and filters work the same way.</p>

	<h3>What the editor needs</h3>

	<ul>
		<li>TypeScript 6 or later installed in your project.</li>
		<li>A <code>tsconfig.json</code> in the folder holding <code>kiln.json</code> or a folder above it, which includes the file with your declarations. The editor uses the nearest one.</li>
	</ul>

	<p>Without them, everything else on this page still works. The editor sees changes to TypeScript files when you save them. The first hover or completion after you open a project can take a moment while TypeScript starts.</p>

	<h3>How the editor follows types</h3>

	<p>The editor works out a value's type from where it comes from:</p>

	<table>
		<thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Type</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>A prop</td><td>Its type in <a href="/docs/tags/props/"><code>props</code></a>, with your types from <code>KilnTypes</code>.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>A global</td><td>Its type in <code>KilnGlobals</code>.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>A call to your function or filter</td><td>What it returns for those arguments. When that is a promise, the value Kiln waits for.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>A built-in filter</td><td>The type the filter gives, such as an item of the list for <code>first</code>, the same list for <code>sort</code>, and a number for <code>length</code>.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>for item in list</code></td><td>An item of the list, or a value of a hash or map. In <code>for key, value in …</code>, the key is a number for a list and a string for a hash.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>An arrow function's parameter in <code>map</code>, <code>filter</code> or <code>sort</code></td><td>An item of the list.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>set name = value</code></td><td>The value's type. A <code>set</code> block holds markup.</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>a.b</code>, <code>a["b"]</code>, <code>a[0]</code></td><td>The property's or item's type.</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<p>A literal's type is its exact value: <code>"tags"</code> is <code>"tags"</code> rather than any string, and <code>2</code> is <code>2</code>. Named arguments are gathered into one hash at the end, so the editor checks them against the function's last parameter.</p>

	<p>When the editor can't tell a value's type, as for an untyped prop or a macro argument, it doesn't guess. You get no property completions for that value, and no warnings about the arguments it's passed to.</p>
{% endblock %}
